# ADVERSARIAL FIXTURE — hostile TEXT in every `block` label surface at once.
#
# This is a VALID document. That is the whole point. Nothing below is a
# syntax probe: every line is ordinary FigDown, and the only unusual thing
# about it is that the STRINGS spell markup, event handlers, a `javascript:`
# URL, a CSS fetch and an `@import`. A `.fd` is untrusted text (core §15),
# and the language's answer to hostile text is not to refuse it — refusing
# `<` would make the language unable to write a figure about XML — but to
# carry it as DATA the whole way to the artifact.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS, and what it cannot.
#
#   PINNED HERE (the `.model.json` golden): every author string arrives in
#   the semantic model BYTE FOR BYTE — the title, the class MEANING, the
#   group and node labels, all three edge-label positions, and every `note=`.
#   `<script>` is four fewer characters than `&lt;script&gt;` and one more
#   token than nothing at all, so a golden that holds the exact string
#   catches an engine that escapes on the way IN, and an engine that strips
#   or sanitises. Escaping belongs to the OUTPUT stage; the model is the
#   source's own text. (Case 800 was written while `note=` had no row in
#   normalize.js and says so; it has one now, and this golden holds six
#   note strings on five different carriers.)
#
#   NOT PINNED HERE: that the same strings reach the ARTIFACT as escaped
#   text and never as markup. That is `gate:safesvg`'s half — it renders
#   this file and reads the output structurally. The two split the claim:
#   the model golden owns "hostile text survives as data", the gate owns
#   "hostile text never becomes markup".
#
#   NOT PINNED ANYWHERE, and said plainly: the SVG has no golden by design
#   (spec §3 — only same source + same renderer version is byte-identical),
#   so nothing here freezes the rendered bytes. `gate:safesvg` asserts a
#   PROPERTY of the output, never its bytes.
figdown 0.3 block
title "</svg><script>alert(1)</script>" note="onload=alert(1) onerror=alert(2)"

# A class label is a MEANING string (`MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE`) and reaches the legend, which is a
# second text surface with its own emitter.
class hostile "<foreignObject><body>x</body></foreignObject>" stroke=#b91c1c

# A group label draws on the band; a node label inside the shape.
group g "javascript:alert(1)" note="url(https://example.invalid/x.png)"
node a "<script>alert(1)</script>" in=g note="@import url(\"https://example.invalid/x.css\");"
node b "\" onclick=\"alert(1)" class=hostile
node c "&amp; & < > \\ done"

# Three edge-label positions, all three hostile. `[ ]` is the bracket form and
# cannot itself contain `]`; the quoted surfaces carry the rest.
edge a [<tail>] -[<mid onload=x>]-> [<head>] b
edge b -> c note="]]> </metadata> <![CDATA["
